Well, note the use of the word "opinion" :) I haven't tried the racket
myself, so I'm just going on what people who use this strategy have said.
You yourself seemed to imply that the racket is an early game gamble.

It could very easily, for example, be against the "best interests" of a
particular character, but reduce *your* number of game turns played between
ascensions.

> Setting aside "ascension" for the moment, does playing the racket
> actually diminish the chances of getting to the quest?  I don't think it
> does.

You've mentioned at least twice that you mind dying in the early game much
less than later on.  Why bring this up if you don't believe your early game
chances are diminished?  (Usually, although not always, when I'm high
enough level to get to the quest, I've found enough gold to get if not full
protection, then at least a number of points worth, without racketeering).

> If you are the sort of player who actually entertains prospects of
> ascending any and every starting character, with a specific percentage
> goal in mind, you're already so experienced that one person's early game
> strategy or another's ought to make little difference.  You know how to
> manage the game clock, you know how to work the movement rules, and you
> know how to anticipate all kinds of stuff.

Personally, I like to play my game as if eventual ascension is the goal for
that character, even if I'm pretty sure I *won't* ascend (e.g. I'm still no
good at the non-combat oriented classes.)

